

**28. Scales of Notation**

**Representation of Numbers**

Any positive real number \(x\) can be uniquely represented in the scale of some integer \(b\) > 1 as

\[x = (A_{m} \cdot \cdot \cdot A_{1}A_{0} \cdot a_{-1}a_{-2} \cdot \cdot \cdot)_{(0)},\]

where every \(A\)4 and _a_-_j_ is one of the integers 0, 1, . . , \(b\) - 1, not all \(A\)1, _a_-_j_ are zero, and _A_- > 0 if \(x\) > 1. There is a one-to-one correspondence between the number and the sequence

\[x = A_{n}b^{m} + \cdot \cdot \cdot +A_{1}b + A_{0} + \frac{\ast}{12}a_{-}b^{-j}\]

where the infinite series converges. The integer \(b\) is called the base or radix of the scale.

The sequence for \(x\) in the scale of \(b\) may terminate, i.e., _a_-_n_-1 = _a_-_n_-2 = . . . =0 for some \(n\) > 1 so that

\[x = (A_{m} \cdot \cdot A_{1}A_{0} \cdot a_{-1}a_{-2} \cdot \cdot \cdot a_{-n})_{(0)};\]

then \(x\) is said to be a finite _b_-adic number.

A sequence which does not terminate may have the property that the infinite sequence _a_-1, _a_-2, . . becomes periodic from a certain digit _a_-_n_(_n_ > 1) on; according as \(n\) = 1 or \(n\) > 1 the sequence is then said to be pure or mixed recurring.

A sequence which neither terminates nor recurs represents an irrational number.

